 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the grid, as each voxel requires one SDF evaluation or occupancy test [G].
--   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the voxel array [G]. For 1D float arrays, this is $4N$ bytes [S1, unofficial]. For 3D bool arrays, this is $N$ bytes due to byte-per-bool storage inefficiency [S2, unofficial].
--   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources [G].
--   **Scaling:** Linear with grid resolution [G]. Power-of-2 allocation may cause memory spikes for grid sizes just above a power of 2 [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Simple indexing and fast random access [G].
--   **Limitations:** Memory inefficient for sparse data because it uses dense storage [S2, unofficial]. Out-of-bounds access returns a sentinel value rather than failing, which may hide bugs if not checked [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to use:** Dense volume representation, SDF computation, and when grid dimensions are known and manageable [G].
--   **When not to use:** Highly sparse scenes where sparse voxel octrees or hash grids would be more memory efficient [G].
+-   **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H \times D)$, where $W, H, D$ are grid dimensions. Each voxel requires a constant number of operations (coordinate transformation, SDF evaluation, storage) [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H \times D)$ for storage. Using `bool` arrays is memory inefficient (1 byte per voxel) compared to bit-packed representations [S2, unofficial].
+-   **Performance:** Performance is heavily dependent on the cost of the SDF evaluation or ray-casting per voxel. For complex meshes, BVH acceleration structures can speed up ray intersection during voxelization [S3, unofficial].
+-   **Strengths:** Simple data structure, easy to implement, supports arbitrary shapes via SDFs [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Limitations:** Memory usage grows cubically with resolution. Out-of-bounds access handling requires explicit checks or large default values [S1, unofficial]. Boolean storage is inefficient [S2, unofficial].
+-   **When to Use:** When a dense, regular grid representation is required for volume rendering or collision detection.
+-   **When Not to Use:** For highly sparse scenes where sparse voxel octrees or instanced rendering are more efficient [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from implementations in Java-based isosurface extraction tools [S1, unofficial] and C++ voxelization utilities [S2, unofficial], as well as Python-based voxelization workflows [S3, unofficial]. It is used in applications requiring the conversion of mesh data into volume data for rendering, solid modeling, or physics simulation [G]. The specific SDF voxelization approach with power-of-2 allocation and sentinel values for out-of-bounds access is documented in the Chickenkeeper/IsosurfaceExtraction repository [S1, unofficial]. The 3D boolean pointer array approach is documented in the antonmartynov/OBJ-Solidizer-And-Voxelizer repository [S2, unofficial]. The shared-grid workflow is documented in the hsafari68/Voxelerate repository [S3, unofficial].
+Voxelization is a fundamental technique in computer graphics, originating from the need to rasterize 3D geometry into a volume representation. Early implementations used simple ray casting or bounding box checks. Modern implementations often leverage Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) for smooth representation and BVH acceleration for efficient mesh intersection [S1, S3, unofficial]. The specific implementations cited here are from open-source projects: IsosurfaceExtraction [S1, unofficial], OBJ-Solidizer-And-Voxelizer [S2, unofficial], and Voxelerate [S3, unofficial].
 
